I closed the campaign as you asked. The test passed: a compressor that has already had one question-answering finetune can stay frozen while only the decoder trains. The higher learning-rate run was not done.

**Check one thing.** Your reply said "do as recommended", but my recommendation had been "yes, run the higher learning-rate test" (about 22 GPU-hours). The rest of your request said "close the campaign here; do not run" that test, so I followed those words. If you meant to run it, delete `campaign_qafreeze/STAND-DOWN` and send a new request.

## Final result
Judge accuracy ↑ (higher is better), seed 42 / seed 43. The difference is the frozen model minus the reference, in points, with a paired 95% interval. 1,500 questions per set. Compression is the same for both models: 8 image tokens per slot, about 123 slots per page.

| set | reference ↑ | frozen compressor, decoder-only training ↑ | difference (pt) | uncompressed ↑ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| documents | .610 / .615 | .615 / .613 | +0.5 [−0.6, +1.6] / −0.1 [−1.1, +0.9] | .809 |
| photos | .679 / .685 | .677 / .679 | −0.3 / −0.5 | .717 |
| MRQA text | .715 / .724 | .726 / .727 | +1.1 / +0.3 | .807 |
| HotpotQA (reported only) | .605 / .591 | .600 / .600 | −0.5 / +0.9 | — |

- Every interval includes 0, so the two models tie on every set.
- **The evaluation can see a gap this size.** A compressor that never had question-answering training, frozen the same way, scores 3.3 points lower on documents at both seeds.
- **What this means:** you can compress a collection once, store the compressed slots, and train only the decoder on them. Building that store is already handed to the pisco chats.
- **Not answered:** whether the higher decoder learning rate would help. It was never run.
- **Cost:** 24.5 GPU-hours of the 55 budgeted, over 28 runs. None failed.

